    }
--expect
171 5 1 0 1 1234 | 0 0 0 0 0 0 | 255 7 0 1 0 2147483647 | 0 0 1 0 0 8 | 0 0 0 0 1 16 || 7 0 0 0 0 99 | 7 5 0 0 0 99 | 7 0 0 0 0 99 | 7 0 1 1 1 99 | 7 0 0 1 1 99 | 7 0 0 0 1 99 | 7 0 0 0 0 99
--expect-why
DERIVED from the header layout stated at the top of `kin/objhdr.kin`: the tag
is bits 31..24, the age 23..21, marked is bit 20, in-remset 19, ascii 18, and
word one is the length. Each group printed is
`ty age marked in-remset ascii len`.

  0xABB40000  ty 0xAB = 171, age 5, marked, not in the remset, ascii
  0x00000000  every field clear
  0xFFE80000  ty 0xFF = 255, age 7, not marked, in the remset, not ascii
  0x00100000  bit 20 alone
  0x00040000  bit 18 alone

THE FIRST AND THIRD GROUPS ARE WHY THE AGE IS MASKED. `obj-age` shifts right
by 21 and then masks to 7, and the mask is not decoration: the tag sits
directly above the age field, so dropping the `& 7` would answer 1373 for the
first group and 2047 for the third instead of 5 and 7. A fixture whose type
tag was zero -- which is what `TY_FREE` is, and what an uninitialised probe
would use -- passes either way, so both groups carry a large tag on purpose.

THE LAST TWO GROUPS SEPARATE THE THREE FLAG BITS, which sit adjacent at 20,
19 and 18. Each is set alone, so a source reading the neighbouring bit answers
false where the expectation is true. The second group is their control: with
every bit clear, all three must be false, which catches the opposite error of
a predicate that ignores its mask and reports the whole word as truthy.

THE THIRD GROUP'S LENGTH IS `0x7FFFFFFF`, THE LARGEST WITH ITS HIGH BIT CLEAR,
and that boundary is deliberate rather than shy. `obj-len` is tagged `U32`
because a FREE BLOCK's size may genuinely set the high bit, and at that point
the three targets stop agreeing about how to PRINT it -- native's `u32` says
4294967295 where both ports' `int` say -1. That is a property of the runtimes
this file is generated for and not of the generated code, so the probe stays
below the boundary and the tag records the fact instead.

AFTER THE `||` ARE THE WRITERS, over ONE address that starts with both words
set to all ones. Every step is a read-modify-write, so each group depends on
the one before it, and a writer that clobbered a neighbouring field would show
up in a LATER group rather than its own.

  write-header(7, 99)   7 0 0 0 0 99   over 0xFFFFFFFF / 0xFFFFFFFF
  set-age(5)            7 5 0 0 0 99   the tag survives
  set-age(8)            7 0 0 0 0 99   8 & 7 = 0, and the tag still survives
  set-marked(true)      7 0 1 1 1 99   remset and ascii were set first
  set-marked(false)     7 0 0 1 1 99   the neighbours survive
  set-in-remset(false)  7 0 0 0 1 99
  set-str-ascii(false)  7 0 0 0 0 99

THE FIRST GROUP IS WHY `write-header` IS NOT A `set`. It runs over a word with
every bit set -- a recycled address -- and must answer with every field but
the type CLEAR. A version that ORed its type in, or that read the old word
first, would inherit an age of 7 and all three flags from whatever lived there
before, and no later group would notice because they all set their own field
explicitly.

THE THIRD GROUP IS THE AGE MASK ON THE WRITE SIDE. `set-age(8)` must answer 0,
not 8: eight does not fit in three bits, and a version that shifted before
masking would write a 1 into bit 24 -- the bottom of the TYPE TAG -- turning
type 7 into type 6 while the age still read 0. The group asserts the tag as
well as the age for exactly that reason.

GROUPS FOUR THROUGH SEVEN CLEAR ONE BIT AT A TIME with both neighbours set,
which is the arrangement that catches an off-by-one in a clear mask. `~(1 <<
20)` written as `~(1 << 19)` leaves group five looking correct -- marked does
go false -- and shows up only as the remset bit vanishing a step early.

NOT COVERED HERE, and named rather than left quiet: `set-forward` and
`forward-target` split a 48-bit address across both header words and need a
u32-to-address widening that ZERO-extends. The three runtimes each spell that
differently -- `as Addr`, `Integer.toUnsignedLong`, `(long)(uint)` -- and
`to-addr` is not it, since on both ports it emits nothing and an `int` would
widen with its sign. `align8`, `slot-addr` and `size-of` are also still
hand-written.
